# PyInstaller
#  Usually these files are written by a single script from a single folder.
#  So often you will want!.spec 'less' in a .gitignore file.
#  Still, there are manyAnaconda environments, and several flavors of Windows
#  and Linux, so Projects might generate varied forms of specs, logs, etc.
*.spec
*.pyzw
*.pyz
*.pyz_extracted/
_MEI*2/

# Jupyter Notebook
.ipynb_checkpoints

# IPython
profile_default/
ipython_config.py

# pyenv
#   For a library or package, you might want to ignore these files since the code is
#   intended to run in a variety of environments; otherwise, check them in:
# .python-version

# pipenv
#   According to the official Pipenv documentation, Pipfile.lock is tracked
#   in version control. But in some cases, it may be desirable to ignore it.
#   For example, if you are developing a library that supports multiple versions of
#   Python and different dependencies.
# Pipfile.lock

# PEP 582; __pypackages__ directory
__pypackages__/

# Celery stuff
celerybeat-schedule
celerybeat.pid

# SageMath parsed files
*.sage.py

